Resend a control-channel packet until it is acknowledged, with gap-free backpressure.

A reliable packet was resent every 10 ms, at most 20 times, then dropped. On TV Wi-Fi a stall
over ~200 ms (a background scan or power save, every few minutes) used up all 20 resends and
the packet was lost. On the control channel that packet carries HID input: a lost one blocks
the host's ordered channel or desyncs the controller, which freezes input. Steam's own client
resends until the packet is acknowledged, which is why a Steam Deck on the same Wi-Fi doesn't
freeze.

Control-channel reliable packets now keep retransmitting until acknowledged or the session ends
(IHS_RetransmissionKeepGoing), with backoff that only applies to the control channel - 10 ms
doubling, capped at 200 ms (IHS_RetransmissionBackoffMs); other channels keep the old fixed
10 ms over 20 attempts. retransmitCount saturates at 255 instead of wrapping, which would reset
the backoff.

"Until acknowledged" must not run away, and it must stay gap-free: a reliable control packet
that is dropped after an id was assigned leaves a permanent hole in the host's ordered channel
and head-of-line-blocks everything after it, including a later full-report resync (a higher id).
So nothing with a packetId is ever dropped. Three mechanisms bound the backlog instead:

- HID backpressure at generation. IHS_SessionHIDSendReport reads the live control backlog and,
  once it is at a soft cap (IHS_RETRANSMISSION_CONTROL_SOFT_CAP, 512, ~2 s of HID at 250 Hz),
  skips the report *before* packing consumes a packetId or an encryption sequence. It resets the
  holders so stale deltas don't pile up, counts it (hidReportsSkipped) and latches a resync.
  Already-queued packets keep retransmitting with backoff until ACKed. When the backlog drains
  back below a watermark (SOFT_CAP/2, hysteresis), the next batch is sent as full reports so the
  host resyncs promptly; the full report is rebuilt from each holder's own last state
  (IHS_HIDReportHolderResyncFull), under the holder lock, never touching managed->device, which
  a concurrent DeviceClose on the receive thread may be freeing.

- A hard cap for non-HID control traffic, which is never throttled. It may exceed the soft cap,
  but at IHS_RETRANSMISSION_CONTROL_HARD_CAP (4096, still well under the int16 wraparound limit)
  a queue attempt Warns and disconnects rather than drop a packet and gap the channel.

- A zombie guard. A resend is handed to the send thread, which re-queues a fresh pending; an ACK
  landing in between marked only the old pending, so the new one would resend forever. Both
  IHS_RetransmissionQueue and the timer now drop a packet a cumulative ACK has already covered
  (the per-channel acked id from 0025).

Session teardown still frees pending items: the timer is destroyed first, running each task's end
callback, then Deinit frees the rest. The backlog gauges (controlPending, controlMaxRetransmit,
hidReportsSkipped) feed the diagnostics in 0024.

